pySMESH: CompositeHexa_3D builds viscous layers (SMESH V9_16_0). This replaces the Phase 4
patch of the same name, which refused them.

Defect: CompositeHexa_3D with a ViscousLayers hypothesis on its solid ends the process with an
access violation, and a ViscousLayers on the solid itself is refused as incompatible. Hexa_3D
hands a solid with more than 6 FACEs to CompositeHexa_3D (StdMeshers_Hexa_3D.cxx:568-575), so
Hexa_3D cannot build layers on such a block either.

Symptom (measured in Phase 4, before the refusal): the unit box, Regular_1D with 4 segments,
Quadrangle_2D, CompositeHexa_3D and ViscousLayers (0.3 thick, 3 layers, factor 1.2) on one
FACE, all on a compound root as pySMESH loads a shape: the compute crashes.

Root cause, measured with instrumented builds:
* Compute takes the hypothesis by name (StdMeshers_CompositeHexa_3D.cxx:858-859) and calls
  StdMeshers_ViscousLayers::Compute (:862). The layer builder takes its hypotheses from the
  algorithm's used hypotheses, filtered by the compatible list (StdMeshers_ViscousLayers.cxx:
  2153-2215), and CompositeHexa_3D lists none, so the builder fails with "ViscousLayers
  hypothesis not found". StdMeshers_ViscousLayers::Compute then returns a null proxy mesh and
  drops that error (:1347-1349), and Compute dereferences the null pointer (:872).
* Made compatible, the layers are built before the side grids are loaded (:862, then
  _QuadFaceGrid::LoadGrid, :1454-1500, and GetNbHoriSegments, :1791-1834). The EDGEs of a side
  FACE that the layers shrank then carry the layer nodes too, so the side grids have more rows
  than the opposite FACEs, and the box grid holds null nodes (16 of 200 on the probe box): the
  compute crashes later. Hexa_3D reads the side structure before the layers and maps every
  grid node to its node in the layer proxy mesh afterwards (StdMeshers_Hexa_3D.cxx:600-612,
  :722-731).
* CheckHypothesis accepts everything (:292-298).
The algorithm is from SMESH 0635c9fc8 (2009-02-17, "Merge from BR_V5_DEV 16Feb09"); its layer
call, for Hexa_3D handing a composite block over, from 0743fa8f5 (2019-04-18, "IPAL54529:
[CKD]: Hexahedron(ijk) fails on a composite block if Viscous Layers assigned"). Upstream master
at 83f8bf482 (2026-09-14) has the same code.

Change, in StdMeshers_CompositeHexa_3D.cxx only:
* the constructor lists ViscousLayers as compatible;
* CheckHypothesis takes one ViscousLayers per solid (two give HYP_INCOMPATIBLE, as in
  Hexa_3D) and runs StdMeshers_ViscousLayers::CheckHypothesis on it;
* Compute finds the six sides and loads their grids from the mesh as it is, computes the grid
  sizes, then builds the layers, and replaces each grid node by its node in the layer proxy
  mesh (_QuadFaceGrid::MapToProxyNodes). Each grid node is looked up again by its id first: the
  layers mesh a side FACE again on a failed smoothing, which removes its nodes; then the
  compute fails with "The viscous layers meshed a side FACE of the block again, so its grid of
  nodes no longer holds" instead of reading a removed node.
On a 2 x 1 x 1 block fused from two unit cubes (10 FACEs) with the layers on its split wall,
the mesh equals Hexa_3D's on the plain 2 x 1 x 1 box, node for node.

Not changed: CompositeHexa_3D with no ViscousLayers: the grids are loaded from the same mesh
in the same order, and the box is built the same way, bit for bit.

